# Web (Next.js App Router)

## Versions

Next.js 16.4 and React 19.3, on Node 24 (`web/.nvmrc`). Tailwind CSS v4, configured in `app/globals.css` (`@import "tailwindcss"; @plugin "@tailwindcss/typography";`); there is no `tailwind.config.js`.

This Next is newer than most training data. Before using an API you aren't sure of, read its page in `web/node_modules/next/dist/docs/` (see `web/AGENTS.md`, which `next dev` writes and keeps). Specifics that matter here:

- `cacheComponents` and `partialPrefetching` are off in `next.config.ts`. Caching uses the fetch-level model (`next: { revalidate, tags }`), documented in `docs/01-app/02-guides/caching-without-cache-components.md`. Don't add `"use cache"`, `cacheLife`, or `cacheTag` unless that setting changes.
- `fetch` is not cached unless you opt in with `next: { revalidate }`.
- `params` and `searchParams` are Promises in pages and layouts: `const { cursor } = await searchParams`.
- Turbopack is the bundler for dev and build; Tailwind runs as a Turbopack CSS loader (`@tailwindcss/turbopack`), not through PostCSS.
- Formatting is Prettier with `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss` (class order); ESLint uses `eslint-config-next` with `eslint-config-prettier` last.

- Server components by default. Add `"use client"` only for state, effects, or browser APIs, and keep those components small and at the leaves.
- Fetch data in server components through the typed API client. Don't call `fetch` against the Go API ad hoc in components.
- The API client lives in one place (e.g. `web/lib/api`), with types matching the Go JSON responses. Don't use `any`.
- Post pages use time-based revalidation (`next: { revalidate: N }`); don't fetch with `no-store` unless the data is truly per-request.

```tsx
// BAD: client component just to fetch
"use client";
export default function Posts() {
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);
  useEffect(() => { fetch("/api/v1/posts").then(/* ... */); }, []);
}

// GOOD: server component using the typed client
import { listPosts } from "@/lib/api";

export default async function Posts() {
  const { items } = await listPosts();
  return <PostList posts={items} />;
}
```

- Search input is a client component that updates the URL query (`?q=`); the results page stays a server component reading `searchParams`.
